**HW5 5.12 (textbook).** Rehashing requires recomputing the hash function of every item, which can be expensive. Suppose objects supply their own hash member function and **each object stores the result the first time the hash is computed**. (a) Show how this scheme applies to the `Employee` class below and its `hash<Employee>` function object. (b) Explain under what circumstances the remembered value remains valid in each Employee.

```cpp
class Employee
{
  public:
    const string & getName( ) const { return name; }
    bool operator==( const Employee & rhs ) const { return getName( ) == rhs.getName( ); }
    bool operator!=( const Employee & rhs ) const { return !( *this == rhs ); }
    // write code here (members to cache the hash value)

  private:
    string name;
    double salary;
    int seniority;
};

template<>
class hash<Employee>
{
  public:
    size_t operator()( const Employee & item )
    {
        static hash<string> hf;
        return hf( item.getName( ) );
    }
};
```

questions.answer:
**(a)** Add a private `size_t` member (plus a flag saying whether it has been computed) that is filled the first time the object is hashed; the functor then just returns the stored value. Because the functor receives a `const Employee &`, the cache members are `mutable`.

```cpp
class Employee
{
  public:
    const string & getName( ) const { return name; }
    void setName( const string & n ) { name = n; hashValid = false; }      // invalidates the cache
    bool operator==( const Employee & rhs ) const { return getName( ) == rhs.getName( ); }
    bool operator!=( const Employee & rhs ) const { return !( *this == rhs ); }

    size_t getHash( ) const                    // computed once, then remembered
    {
        if( !hashValid )
        {
            static hash<string> hf;
            hashVal = hf( name );              // the raw, unscaled hash
            hashValid = true;
        }
        return hashVal;
    }

  private:
    string name;
    double salary;
    int seniority;
    mutable size_t hashVal = 0;
    mutable bool   hashValid = false;
};

template<>
class hash<Employee>
{
  public:
    size_t operator()( const Employee & item ) const { return item.getHash( ); }
};
```

**(b)** The remembered value is valid as long as the **name** (the member the hash is computed from, and the member `operator==` compares) does not change, and as long as the hash function itself is unchanged. If the name changes the stored value is wrong (the setter above resets it). Professor's remark: it is hard to use this *across a rehash* when the stored number is the position in the table - rehashing changes every element's position/hash value. If we store the unscaled `size_t` returned by `hash<Employee>` and let `myhash` apply `% theLists.size()` afterwards, the stored value survives a rehash (only the cheap modulo is recomputed), which is the point of the scheme.

