questions.answer:
(a) **Naive:** two nested loops over all pairs (i < j) testing a[i] + a[j] == x: n(n-1)/2 tests, **O(n^2)** time, O(1) extra space.

(b) **Sorting:** sort a copy, O(n log n) (e.g. std::sort); then place one index at each end and move them: if the sum is too small advance the left index, if too large move the right index back, stop when they meet: O(n). Total **O(n log n)** (dominated by the sort), O(1) extra space if sorted in place (or O(n) for a copy).

(c) **Hashing:** one pass; for each element v look for x - v among the elements already seen in an unordered_set, then insert v. Each `count` and `insert` is O(1) average, so **O(n) average time**, O(n) extra space. Worst case (all elements in one bucket) each operation is O(n): **O(n^2) worst**.

(d)
```cpp
#include <unordered_set>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

bool hasPairWithSum( const vector<int> & a, long long x )
{
    unordered_set<long long> seen;               // values we have already passed
    for( int v : a )
    {
        if( seen.count( x - v ) )                // is the complement among earlier elements?
            return true;
        seen.insert( v );                        // insert AFTER the test: an element may not pair with itself
    }
    return false;
}
```
The complement is tested before v is inserted, so two equal values (x = 2v) are found only if v occurs twice. Cost: n iterations, each one `count` and one `insert`: O(1) average each -> O(n) average.

