You want `unordered_set<Point>` for the type below. (a) Write what is needed so that it compiles: the equality operator and a specialization of `hash<Point>` (the function object of textbook Section 5.3) that combines both coordinates. (b) Then show the alternative of Fig 5.23, passing a hash functor as a template argument instead of specializing `hash`. (c) Which property must the hash function have with respect to `operator==`?

```cpp
struct Point
{
    int x;
    int y;
    // write code here
};
```

questions.answer:
(a) Specialization of `hash`:

```cpp
#include <functional>
#include <unordered_set>
using namespace std;

struct Point
{
    int x;
    int y;
    bool operator==( const Point & rhs ) const { return x == rhs.x && y == rhs.y; }
};

namespace std
{
    template<>
    struct hash<Point>
    {
        size_t operator()( const Point & p ) const
        {
            static hash<int> hf;
            return hf( p.x ) * 37 + hf( p.y );     // use both coordinates (a Horner-style combination)
        }
    };
}

unordered_set<Point> pts;           // pts.insert( Point{ 3, 4 } );  pts.count( Point{ 3, 4 } );
```

(b) Hash functor passed as a template argument (as in Fig 5.23, which also passes the equality):

```cpp
struct PointHash
{
    size_t operator()( const Point & p ) const { return hash<int>( )( p.x ) * 37 + hash<int>( )( p.y ); }
};
struct PointEqual
{
    bool operator()( const Point & a, const Point & b ) const { return a.x == b.x && a.y == b.y; }
};

unordered_set<Point, PointHash, PointEqual> pts2;
```

(c) **Equal objects must have equal hash values** (a == b implies hash(a) == hash(b)): the hash may only use the members that `==` compares (here both x and y). Otherwise equal points could be sent to different buckets and `find` would miss them. (Unequal objects may collide - that only costs time.) Hashing only p.x would still be correct but would put all points of a vertical line into one bucket.

